<TopicPage slug="subjects/international-relations/foundational/frameworks-for-understanding-international-relations">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    MIT's 17.41 and the textbook it is built around — Frieden, Lake & Schultz's
    <em>World Politics: Interests, Interactions, Institutions</em> — reduce the chaos of
    world politics to three lenses. <strong>Interests</strong> are what actors want: power,
    security, wealth, ideology. <strong>Interactions</strong> are what happens when their
    choices collide: situations of cooperation or bargaining. <strong>Institutions</strong>
    are the rules that structure both — who may do what, with whom, and who verifies it.
    The method of the course is to take one real puzzle and look at it through each lens in
    turn, refusing the lazy answer that a single motive "explains" a war, a treaty or a
    trade dispute.
  </p>

  <h2>How the rules got there</h2>
  <Widget no="1" caption="the slow construction of the international order — sovereignty, then institutions" widget="timeline" config={irTimeline} />
  <p>
    Read the timeline through the three lenses. Westphalia and Vienna set the deep rule —
    sovereignty and balance. The League, UN, GATT and WTO are attempts to build
    institutions that make cooperation enforceable where interests alone would leave states
    trapped in rivalry. The Paris Agreement shows the modern version of the problem:
    universal membership, minimal enforcement.
  </p>

  <h2>The three lenses, one at a time</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Interests:</strong> states (and firms, NGOs, voters) hold preferences over outcomes. Much apparent "irrationality" in foreign policy is just clashing interests — or clashing interests <em>inside</em> one government.</li>
    <li><strong>Interactions:</strong> some situations are coordination (everyone gains from agreeing on one standard); others are collective-action dilemmas (climate, arms races — each prefers to free-ride); most hard cases are bargaining over who gets how much of what.</li>
    <li><strong>Institutions:</strong> rules lower the cost of making and keeping agreements — they gather information, lengthen the shadow of the future, and raise the cost of defection. They have no world police, which is exactly why their design matters.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The lenses also fix who counts as an actor. The state is the primary one —
    Westphalia's legacy — but the framework extends without strain to international
    organisations, firms, NGOs and militaries, each with interests of its own and each
    interacting through the same logic. And it scales: the same puzzle can be read at the
    level of the individual leader, the domestic coalition, or the international system.
    What the framework refuses is the single-actor shortcut of saying "France did X" —
    interests are held by someone in particular, and finding that someone is half the
    analysis.
  </p>

  <h2>The sharpest tool: the bargaining model of war</h2>
  <Widget no="2" caption="war as bargaining failure — Fearon's logic, step by step" widget="equationStep" config={bargainingCfg} />
  <p>
    This is the frameworks approach at full power: a puzzle (why do costly wars happen when
    deals exist?), decomposed into interests (each wants the whole prize), interactions (a
    bargaining problem), and institutions (missing or failed ones). It also predicts where
    peace comes from: transparency that closes information gaps, and designs that make
    commitments credible.
  </p>
  <p>
    It also shows what institutions can and cannot do. A well-built institution does three
    things: it collects and certifies information (monitoring, verification), it lengthens
    the shadow of the future by making today's defection costly tomorrow, and it lowers the
    transaction costs of negotiating among dozens of states at once. What it cannot do is
    manufacture the will to comply — which is why the League's covenant and the WTO's
    dispute mechanism are such different success stories, and why the Paris Agreement's
    transparency was engineered to work even without enforcement.
  </p>

  <h2>Applying the lenses: climate change</h2>
  <p>
    Take one modern puzzle and test the toolkit. <em>Interests:</em> every state prefers a
    stable climate, but each also prefers others to pay for it — divergent development
    levels make the split bitterly contested. <em>Interactions:</em> a classic
    collective-action problem, not a misunderstanding. <em>Institutions:</em> Paris 2015
    builds transparency (pledge-and-review) precisely to attack the information and
    commitment problems — weakly, and everyone knows it, which is the honest lesson of
    institutions: they shape outcomes only as strongly as their design and their members'
    interests allow.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> MIT OpenCourseWare 17.41 Introduction to International Relations (course calendar and readings); Frieden, Lake & Schultz, World Politics: Interests, Interactions, Institutions; Fearon, "Rationalist Explanations for War", International Organization 49(3) (1995); Keohane, After Hegemony (1984).</p>
</TopicPage>
